<% content_for :title, "About — MindCanvas" %>
<% content_for :sidebar_active, "about" %>

<%# No container of its own: the layout's <main> owns the measure and gutters,
    so this page lines up with every other one for free.

    Shape of the page, top to bottom: what MindCanvas is -> why it exists ->
    how it works -> who built it -> where he's worked -> how to reach him.
    The platform comes first and the person second: a reader landing here is
    deciding whether to write on MindCanvas, not reading a CV. Connect stays
    the closing section — it's the one action left once everything else has
    made its case.

    Two rules this page is held to:
      * Every hard fact about Sayan — employers, years, links — comes from
        his own LinkedIn profile. Nothing here is invented, and nothing is
        lifted from the stories.
      * No duration is written as a literal number. Anything time-based goes
        through years_of_experience so the page never goes stale. %>

<%# ---- 1. What this is --------------------------------------------------- %>
<div data-reveal-item class="pb-2">
  <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">About</p>
  <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 sm:text-4xl">
    A quiet place to write, and to be read.
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
    MindCanvas is a writing platform. Open a draft the moment a thought arrives,
    keep it to yourself for as long as you need, and publish it to everyone when
    it's finally saying what you meant.
  </p>
</div>

<%# ---- 2. Why it exists -------------------------------------------------- %>
<section data-reveal-item class="surface-float mt-8 rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8">
  <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Why it exists</p>
  <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
    <p>
      Most half-formed ideas die in a notes app. Not because they were bad, but
      because the gap between "thought" and "something worth publishing" is where
      the friction lives — the setup, the formatting, the vague dread of putting
      an unfinished thing somewhere public.
    </p>
    <p>
      MindCanvas removes the middle of that. Writing starts private and stays
      private until you say otherwise, so a draft costs you nothing. Publishing is
      one decision, made once, whenever you're ready — and what's published lands
      in a shared library anyone can read, next to what everyone else is working
      through.
    </p>
    <p>
      No topic belongs here more than any other. Craft, work, the thing you got
      wrong last week, the rabbit hole you're three days into — it's all writing,
      and it all counts.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<%# ---- 3. How it works --------------------------------------------------- %>
<%# These four cards describe what the app actually does today: drafts are
    private, publishing is a switch, My stories splits into published/drafts
    tabs, and All stories is the public shelf. If that behaviour changes, this
    section changes with it — it is documentation, not decoration. %>
<section class="mt-10">
  <div data-reveal-item>
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">The shape of it</p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">How writing here works</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <% [
      ["Start in private", "Every piece begins as a draft that only you can see. Leave it for an hour or for a month — nothing is published until you choose to publish it."],
      ["Publish in one move", "When a draft is ready, one switch sends it out. No separate export, no second copy to keep in sync."],
      ["Your shelf, two tabs", "My stories holds everything you've written, published work and drafts side by side, so nothing gets lost between them."],
      ["A shared library", "All stories is the front page — everything published on MindCanvas, newest first, open to anyone who wants to read."]
    ].each do |title, blurb| %>
      <div data-reveal-item data-reveal-top class="surface-float rounded-2xl p-5 sm:p-6">
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100"><%= title %></p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300"><%= blurb %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <%= render ButtonComponent.new(href: published_posts_path, variant: :primary) do %>
      Read what's published
    <% end %>
    <% unless user_signed_in? %>
      <%= render ButtonComponent.new(href: new_user_registration_path, variant: :secondary) do %>
        Start writing
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<%# ---- 4. Who built it --------------------------------------------------- %>
<section data-reveal-item class="surface-float mt-10 rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-start">
    <%# transition/hover live on this wrapper rather than AvatarComponent
        itself — the component is shared everywhere else (post bylines, the
        header) and shouldn't tilt on hover there. Ease-spring is the same
        curve the header's theme toggle uses, so the one playful gesture on
        the page still speaks the app's existing motion language. %>
    <div class="shrink-0 rounded-full transition-transform duration-500 ease-spring hover:-rotate-3 hover:scale-105">
      <%= render AvatarComponent.new(name: "Sayan Sinha", size: :xl) %>
    </div>

    <div class="min-w-0">
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Who built it</p>
      <h2 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Sayan Sinha</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
        Software engineer &middot; Kolkata, India
      </p>

      <div class="mt-5 space-y-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
        <p>
          I'm a software engineer, and a properly passionate one. <%= years_of_experience %>+ years in,
          I still care about the same thing I did on day one: building things that are
          quick, sturdy, and clean enough that whoever opens the file next doesn't groan.
        </p>
        <p>
          I've led teams through the unglamorous parts of shipping, and I've worked
          shoulder-to-shoulder with hardware and firmware people to get IoT products
          out of the door. That seam — where software finally has to meet something
          physical — is where I've learned the most.
        </p>
        <p>
          Somewhere along the way I picked up the Scrum Master seat too, less by plan
          than by necessity — someone had to keep a team honest about scope and sane
          about pace, and it turned out to suit me. I'd rather spend an afternoon
          unblocking a teammate than spend it polishing something alone, and most of
          what I know about shipping well I learned from watching a team get stuck and
          figuring out why.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lately that same restlessness has pointed at AI — not as a shortcut around
          writing code, but as another tool worth actually understanding, the way Rails
          or Kafka once were. I'm still mostly learning this one in public: trying
          things, getting a fair share of them wrong, and writing down what's left once
          the dust settles.
        </p>
        <p>
          MindCanvas came out of wanting a writing home that didn't get in the way. I
          use it myself, and it's open to anyone who wants the same thing. No pressure
          to publish, no audience watching a draft take shape — just a place to get a
          thought out of your head before it disappears, and to let it go public
          whenever you're ready and not a moment before.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
  <div data-reveal-item data-reveal-top class="surface-float rounded-2xl p-5">
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Experience</p>
    <%# Counts up from zero the moment it scrolls into view — see
        counter_controller.js. Purely cosmetic: the number rendered here is
        already the real, final value, so anyone without JS (or with
        prefers-reduced-motion set) just sees it sitting still. %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100" data-controller="counter"><%= years_of_experience %>+ years</p>
  </div>
  <div data-reveal-item data-reveal-top class="surface-float rounded-2xl p-5">
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Works on</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Web &amp; IoT</p>
  </div>
  <div data-reveal-item data-reveal-top class="surface-float rounded-2xl p-5">
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Based in</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Kolkata, India</p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ---- 5. Where I've worked ----------------------------------------------- %>
<%# Names only, and no schooling. Titles, dates and degrees belong on a CV —
    someone deciding whether to write here does not need an employment
    timeline, only a reason to trust that the person behind it has shipped
    software before. %>
<section class="mt-10">
  <div data-reveal-item>
    <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">Experience</p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Where I've worked</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <% [
      "Rently",
      "Bit Canny Technologies",
      "Kreeti Technologies"
    ].each do |company| %>
      <div data-reveal-item data-reveal-top class="surface-float rounded-2xl p-5">
        <p class="font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100"><%= company %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<%# ---- 6. Let's connect ---------------------------------------------------
    The closing section on purpose: it's the one thing left to do once the
    page has made its case, not a sign-off competing with the rest. Three
    networks, so the copy can no longer single one out as "the fastest way"
    — the row of marks makes the choice, not the sentence above it.

    Plain anchors rather than ButtonComponent: its SIZES are padding built
    for a text label (px-4 alongside a fixed height), which turns lopsided
    the moment the label goes away and only the icon is left centered inside
    it. A fixed h-10 w-10 circle is what an icon-only control actually wants
    — the same shape as the header's menu/theme icon-buttons — so each mark
    gets an accessible name via aria-label instead of visible text. %>
